    <title>Rethinking the Evaluation of LLM Fact-Checkers</title>
    <updated>2026-10-21T22:36:44Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30504v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30504v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) are widely used to answer factual questions, but their answers can contain errors and must be fact-checked claim by claim. Yet the resulting score depends on three design decisions: which questions the claims answer, under what conditions each claim is judged, and which sources count as evidence. No existing benchmark specifies all three. We introduce JSFactCheckBench, a Japanese benchmark that specifies all three, with 1,913 claims from LLM answers to questions about misleading information that circulated on Japanese social media, each annotated for factuality at a reference time and for importance to the question, using evidence from preselected Web sources. Across six LLMs, all verify claims directly answering the question less accurately than supplementary claims, a gap that aggregate scores obscure. Scores also drop when models must locate the relevant content in longer or multiple sources and, for all four open models, when the surrounding question and response are added. These weaknesses are visible only because the benchmark records the conditions behind each label. A benchmark that leaves them implicit cannot reveal them.</summary>
